# scripts/verify_release.py hashes the exact bytes of every released artifact,
# and release-manifest.json records the LF bytes that are committed. With
# core.autocrlf=true and no attributes file, a Windows checkout inflates those
# files to CRLF, so the audit reported 19 of its 25 hashes as mismatched while
# the repository itself was intact: the blob in HEAD already matched. Pinning
# the checkout to LF makes the working tree byte-identical to the committed
# blob on every platform, which is what the manifest is asserting.
#
# Every tracked blob is LF-only today, so this changes no content. It also
# protects the six .sh files, which break outright when checked out with CRLF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Byte-audited models and other binaries must never reach the text filter.
# demo-screenshot.png happens to contain CR LF byte pairs inside its image
# data, which is exactly the case content detection should not have to guess.
*.ubj binary
*.png binary
*.mp4 binary
*.zip binary
